<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert II of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">972–1031</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Robert the Pious</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert II, called the Pious, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 996 to 1031, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a>, with whom he was crowned in 987. He fought for years to win the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> after the death of his uncle Henry the Great in 1002, securing it by about 1005. His marriages brought conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, and his son Henry I succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-ii-of-france.jpg" alt="Robert II of France" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Odysseus numismatique · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_II_the_pious,_denier_-_Laon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
